<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="multi-var-integrals-complete">
    <title>Integrals of Multivariable Functions (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Integrating <m>f(x)</m> is another important concept from calculus I. How can we extend
                        integration to multivariable functions?
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Double Integrals</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                      Consider a function <m>f(x,y)</m>. A natural approach to integrating such a function is to repeat our
                      approach for partial derivatives: 
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We can make sense of integrating <m>f(x,y)</m> with respect to a single variable by holding one
                        variable constant. For example, if <m>f(x,y) = xy^2</m>, then
                        <md>
                            \int_0^1 xy^2 \quad dx = y^2 \int_0^1 x \quad dx = \left. \frac{1}{2}x^2y^2 \right|_0^1 = \frac{1}{2}y^2
                        </md>
                        and
                        <md>
                            \int_0^2 xy^2 \quad dy = x \int_0^2 y^2 \quad dy = \left. \frac{1}{3}y^3x \right|_0^2 = \frac{8}{3}x
                        </md>
                        Note that integration with respect to <m>x</m> (respectively <m>y</m>) results in a function <m>y</m> ( respectively <m>x</m>). 
                        Therefore, these are expressions we could integrate again:
                        <md>
                           \int_0^2 \int_0^1 xy^2 \quad dx dy = \int_0^2 \frac{1}{2}y^2 \quad dy = \left. \frac{1}{6}y^3 \right|_0^2 = \frac{8}{6} 
                        </md>
                        and
                        <md>
                            \int_0^1\int_0^2 xy^2 \quad dy dx = \int_0^1 \frac{8}{3}x \quad dx = \left. \frac{8}{6}x^2 \right|_0^1 = \frac{8}{6} 
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Notes and Notation: Double Integrals</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                A general notation for a double integral is 
                                <md>
                                    \iint_D f(x,y) \quad dA
                                </md>
                                When all bounds are constants (such as in our example), we call <m>D</m> a <term>rectangular region</term>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>f(x,y)</m> is nice on <m>D</m>, then
                                <md>
                                    \iint_D f(x,y) \quad dxdy = \iint_D f(x,y) \quad dydx
                                </md>
                                In some cases, one order is much easier than the other!
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Geometrically, <m>\iint_D f(x,y) \quad dA</m> represents the (signed) volume between
                                <m>f(x,y)</m> and the <m>x-y</m> plane over the region <m>D</m>. Some integrals can be recognized 
                                geometrically and avoid computation. For example, what is the value of <m>\int_1^5 \int_0^3 1 \quad dx dy</m> without
                                making any computation?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Separable Integrals</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f(x,y) = g(x) \cdot h(y)</m>, then
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <m>
                        \displaystyle
                         \int_c^d \int_a^b f(x,y) \quad dx dy = 
                        </m>
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow> \amp=\int_c^d \int_a^b g(x) h(y) \quad dx dy </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= \int_c^d h(y) \left(\int_a^b g(x) \quad dx\right) \quad dy </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= \left(\int_a^b g(x) \quad dx \right) \cdot \left(\int_c^d h(y) \quad dy\right) </mrow>
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <title>Practice: Double Integrals</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                    Compute the following double integrals.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
              <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle \int_1^2 \int_2^4 6xy^2 \quad dxdy</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        You can separate this integral, or compute as with the first example. Either method should give an answer of
                        <m>84</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
              </task>

              <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle \int_{-5}^4 \int_0^3 2x - 4y^3 \quad dydx</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        You cannot separate this integral, but you could compute as either <m>dydx</m> or <m>dxdy</m>. Either computation
                        should give <m>-756</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
              </task>
                
            </exercise>
</page>

<page>
        <exercise workspace="6in">
        <title>Triple Integrals</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                We can extend double integrals of <m>f(x,y)</m> naturally to triple integrals of <m>f(x,y,z)</m>. Let's
                make some analogous observations:
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The general notation for triple integrals is
                            <md>
                                \iiint_D f(x,y,z) \quad dV
                            </md>
                            
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For nice functions <m>f(x,y,z)</m> on <m>D</m>, we can still choose our order of integration.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            In general, <m> \iiint_D f(x,y,z) \quad dV</m> is difficult to visualize, but
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m> \iiint_D 1 \quad dV</m> is just a way to represent the volume of the 
                                        region <m>D</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The typically compute important properties of three-dimensional objects
                                        (like mass, electromagnitism, etc.).
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
</page>

<page>
        <exercise workspace="6in">
        <title>Example: Triple Integrals</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute <m>\displaystyle \iiint_D 8xyz \quad dV</m>, where <m>D</m> is the region given by
                <md>
                    <mrow> 2 \leq \amp x \leq 3 </mrow>
                    <mrow> 1 \leq \amp y \leq 2 </mrow>
                    <mrow> 0 \leq \amp z \leq 1 </mrow>
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                You can compute this integral in any order, all of which should give an answer of <m>15</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
</page>



   

       
    </worksheet>
